==Trivia==
*Every even-numbered stage is a copy of the stage before it, except the main threat is different.
*The Japanese names of Furious Field and Mirage Meadow are puns. Both have the word ''kyozō'' in their names, but written with different kanji to form different meanings. Furious Field spells ''kyozō'' as 巨象, meaning "[[Le'mammoth|giant elephant]]", whereas Mirage Meadow uses 虚像, referring to a [[wikipedia:Virtual image|virtual image]].
*[[Crag of Monte Cristo]] and [[Beast of Monte Cristo]] are references to the novel [[wikipedia:The Count of Monte Cristo|''The Count of Monte Cristo'']]. Within this context, [[THE FOLIVOREAN]] in Beast of Monte Cristo may represent the [[wikia:gankutsuou:The Count of Monte Cristo (character)|titular count]] of the anime ''[[wikipedia:Gankutsuou: The Count of Monte Cristo|Gankutsuou: The Count of Monte Cristo]]'', who turned blue after being demonically possessed by [[wikia:gankutsuou:The King of the Cavern|The King of the Cavern]]. Witch explains why [[King Wahwah]] is in [[Blessings in Sand]].
*[[Puffsley's Comet]] and [[Mr. Puffington]] are in [[Misfortune in Sand]] for the sake of a pun that was lost in translation. In Japanese, the stage's name is Sareki o Gyūjiru Fugū na Mono (砂礫を牛耳る不遇な者, "The Unfortunate One Ruling Over Pebbles"); the joke is that 不遇 ''fugū'' (misfortune) sounds almost like 河豚 ''fugu'' (pufferfish). Misfortune also links with Greed the sin of [[Puffsley's  Comet]]. That gives us the reasoning of why it has this word.

